Below is a production-ready Nest.js microservice that ingests an XML file into memory for fast lookups, with an optional MongoDB backend if you later decide persistence or larger datasets are needed. It’s built on Express via Nest, includes a health endpoint, supports hot-reload on XML file changes, and is containerized.

Notes
- In-memory mode: default, fast, no DB required. Loads XML at startup and builds indexes for O(1) lookups by configured fields.
- Mongo mode: toggle with USE_MONGO=true. Uses a schemaless collection (strict: false) so you can persist any XML structure.
- Config-driven: You can point to the XML path and specify where in the parsed object the records live and which fields to index.
- Endpoints:
  - GET /health
  - GET /lookup?field=FIELD&value=VALUE
  - GET /lookup/fields
  - GET /lookup/count
  - POST /lookup/reload (requires X-Admin-Token = RELOAD_TOKEN)

How it works
- At startup:
  - If USE_MONGO=false, parses the XML file (XML_PATH), extracts an array of objects from XML_RECORD_PATH (dot path), and builds in-memory indexes for fields in LOOKUP_INDEX_FIELDS (+ PRIMARY_KEY_FIELD).
  - If USE_MONGO=true, loads from Mongo using MONGO_URI/MONGO_DB/MONGO_COLLECTION and runs queries against it.
- Optional file watch:
  - ENABLE_FILE_WATCH=true uses chokidar to auto-reload on file changes.
- Containerized with a multi-stage Dockerfile and optional docker-compose.

